 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Function",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Two independent non-inverting buffers, Y = A. Each input is a Schmitt trigger: it switches high at VT+ and back low at the lower VT-, and the difference, 0.3 V to 1.4 V depending on supply, is hysteresis that improves noise immunity."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply and level shifting",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Runs from 1.65 V to 5.5 V. The inputs take up to 5.5 V at any valid VCC, so the part shifts levels down: 5 V to 3.3 V, or 5 V or 3.3 V to 1.8 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Partial power-down (Ioff)",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "With VCC at 0 V the Ioff circuit turns the outputs off, so input and output pins may carry voltage without current flowing back into the unpowered part (Ioff ±10 µA max at 5.5 V). This allows live insertion, partial power-down and back-drive."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output drive",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "CMOS outputs with balanced drive, ±24 mA at 3.3 V. The drive is strong enough to exceed the absolute-maximum current in bus contention. Paralleled outputs give more drive; with a light load their fast edges can ring, so routing and load matter. Load current limit: 50 mA per output, 100 mA for the whole part. A series resistor at an output slows its edges or limits its current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Ground bounce and undershoot",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Typical output ground bounce VOLP below 0.8 V and typical VOH undershoot VOHV above 2 V, at VCC = 3.3 V, TA = 25 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply bypass",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Bypass VCC with 0.1 µF placed as close to the pin as possible. Several capacitors in parallel may be used to filter different noise frequencies. Any supply between the recommended minimum and maximum works."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Unused inputs",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "No input may float: an open CMOS input sits at an undefined level and leaves the logic in an undefined state. Each unused input is tied to VCC or GND, whichever suits the circuit."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Robustness",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Latch-up performance above 100 mA per JESD 78, class II. ESD to JESD 22: 2000 V human-body model, 1000 V charged-device model. Store and handle the part with its leads shorted or in conductive foam."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Packages",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "SOT-23 (DBV), SC70 (DCK), SON 1.45 mm × 1.00 mm (DRY), SON 1.00 mm × 1.00 mm (DSF) and NanoFree DSBGA (YZP), a die-size ball grid array in which the die itself is the package. All six-pin with the same pin numbering; MSL level 1 at 260 °C peak."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Other qualified versions",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "An automotive version qualified to AEC-Q100, SN74LVC2G17-Q1, and an enhanced-product version for defense, aerospace and medical use, SN74LVC2G17-EP, are separate devices."
  }
 ],
